🧠 HOTEL ROOM BOOKING SYSTEM — FULL VIVA BREAKDOWN
1. 📦 Import
import java.util.Scanner;
📌 Overall meaning:

Imports Scanner class to take user input.

🧩 Terms:
import → includes external class
java.util → package
Scanner → input class
⭐ Alternatives:
BufferedReader
Console
2. 🧱 Class
class HotelBookingSystem
📌 Overall meaning:

Defines program structure for hotel booking system.

🧩 Terms:
class → blueprint
HotelBookingSystem → user-defined name
3. 🚪 Main method
public static void main(String[] args)
📌 Overall meaning:

Program execution starts here.

🧩 Terms:
public → accessible by JVM
static → no object needed
void → no return
main → entry point
4. 📥 Scanner object
Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
📌 Overall meaning:

Takes input from user.

⭐ Alternatives:
BufferedReader (faster)
Scanner alternatives in advanced Java
5. 🏨 Hotel structure
int floors = 3;
int rooms = 4;
📌 Overall meaning:

Defines hotel structure: 3 floors, 4 rooms each.

🧩 Terms:
int → integer type
variables define structure size
⭐ Alternatives:
user input instead of fixed values
6. 🟩 2D Array
boolean[][] hotel = new boolean[floors][rooms];
📌 Overall meaning:

Creates a grid of rooms (floors × rooms).

🧩 Terms:
boolean → true/false
[][] → 2D array (matrix)
new → memory allocation
🎯 Meaning of values:
false → available room
true → booked room
⭐ Alternatives:
int[][] (0 = free, 1 = booked)
object-based Room class (advanced OOP)
7. 🔁 do-while loop
do { ... } while (choice != 3);
📌 Overall meaning:

Menu keeps running until user exits.

⭐ Alternatives:
while(true)
for(;;)
8. 📋 Menu display
System.out.println("1. View Rooms");
📌 Overall meaning:

Shows options to user.

9. 🎯 choice input
choice = sc.nextInt();
📌 Overall meaning:

Reads user menu selection.

10. 👁️ View Rooms (Option 1)
for (int i = 0; i < floors; i++)
📌 Overall meaning:

Loops through floors.

🧩 Terms:
for → loop
i → row index
for (int j = 0; j < rooms; j++)
📌 Overall meaning:

Loops through rooms in each floor.

if (hotel[i][j])
    System.out.print("B ");
else
    System.out.print("A ");
📌 Overall meaning:

Checks room status:

true → Booked (B)
false → Available (A)
🧩 Terms:
if → condition
hotel[i][j] → 2D array access
⭐ Alternatives:
use symbols like X / O
or numbers (1/0)
11. 🏷️ Book Room (Option 2)
int f = sc.nextInt();
int r = sc.nextInt();
📌 Overall meaning:

Takes floor and room number.

🚨 Validation
if (f < 1 || f > floors || r < 1 || r > rooms)
📌 Overall meaning:

Checks if input is valid.

🧩 Terms:
|| → OR operator
< > → comparison operators
⭐ Alternatives:
separate nested if checks
🚫 Already booked check
else if (hotel[f - 1][r - 1])
📌 Overall meaning:

Checks if room already booked.

🟢 Booking
hotel[f - 1][r - 1] = true;
📌 Overall meaning:

Marks room as booked.

12. 🚪 Exit option
else if (choice == 3)
📌 Overall meaning:

Stops program.

13. ❌ Invalid choice
else
📌 Overall meaning:

Handles wrong input.

🧠 OVERALL FLOW

👉 User opens system
👉 Chooses:

View rooms → shows A/B grid
Book room → marks room true
Exit → stops program
⚡ CONCEPTUAL VIVA QUESTIONS (IMPORTANT)
❓ Why use boolean 2D array?

👉 To represent grid of rooms with two states: booked or available.

❓ What does hotel[i][j] mean?

👉 i = floor, j = room number.

❓ Why use 2D array instead of 1D?

👉 Because hotel structure has rows and columns.

❓ What is meaning of true/false here?

👉 true = booked
👉 false = available

❓ Why subtract 1 (f - 1)?

👉 Because array index starts from 0, but user input starts from 1.

❓ What is use of || operator?

👉 It checks multiple invalid conditions (OR logic).

❓ Why do we use loops?

👉 To traverse all floors and rooms.

❓ What is real-world use?

👉 Hotel booking systems, movie seat booking, train seat reservation.

❓ Why do we use Scanner?

👉 To take input from user.

❓ What happens if invalid input entered?

👉 Program shows “Invalid floor or room number”.

❓ Why use nested loops?

👉 Because we are working with 2D structure.

🧠 FINAL ONE-LINE SUMMARY

“This program simulates a hotel booking system using a 2D boolean array to track room availability, allowing users to view, book, and validate rooms using a menu-driven approach.”
